  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Monday)\nIssued at 905 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThe general upper level setup will consist of a ridging west and \ntroughing east pattern over the greater North American domain. This \npattern was being kept in place by Omega-style upper blocking over \nthe northern Pacific basin. At the beginning of the extended, upper \ntroughing will be in place off of the west coast (the eastern anchor \nof the upper block), with strong upper level ridging situated over \nthe southern Great Plains. A passing upper low over the central \nOntario and Quebec region will deepen, and help amplify upper \ntroughing from the eastern Great Lakes to northern Florida. This will\nplace the Tennessee Valley in a WNW-ESE flow from the late week into\nearly next week. \n\nA northwesterly upper level flow across this area, especially if it\nis disturbance laden, is known for bringing showers and thunderstorms \nto the Valley. Convection in these regimes can be accompanied by \nstrong winds. For this area, a few complications to that simplistic \nview. First, a cold front should be south of the area by late Thu. \nMoisture from Tropical Storm Bertha should be streaming back to the \nNE, with some of it impacting the area Fri into Sat. Along with lower \nlevel tropical moisture, some of the extended guidance was suggesting\nthat upper level support moving across the area from late Fri into \nSun could bring more thunderstorms to the area. It is way too early \nto discern if this convection could become more significant, given \nmuch model uncertainty this far out. With a main synoptic front to\nour south and with some shower/storm chances, daily high/low \ntemperatures should trend a bit milder, with highs Fri/Sat in the mid\n80s to around 90. Warmer conditions look return for the start of the\nnew week, with highs from the upper 80s to lower 90s Sun, and low/mid \n90s next Mon. Lows Thu night should range in the mid/upper 60s, with \nmainly lower 70s for the Fri - Sun night period.",
